From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 23:15:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Drop a stale EXPLAIN ANALYZE flag when a query starts

QueryProfiler::StartQuery() sets is_explain_analyze when the statement
it starts is an EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and EndQuery() clears it, but only for
a running profiler. Two paths leave the flag set with no EndQuery() to
clear it:

* A query that fails is rolled back in EndQueryInternal() before the
  profiler is ended, and TransactionContext::Rollback() calls
  QueryProfiler::Reset(), which stops the profiler. EndQuery() then
  finds it idle and returns early. This covers an error raised while the
  query runs, a prepared statement that fails to rebind, and a table
  dropped between prepare and execute, through Query() and through a
  prepared statement alike. Inside an explicit transaction nothing is
  rolled back before EndQuery(), and nothing leaks.
* CreatePreparedStatementInternal() calls StartQuery() with the flag
  while preparing, without starting the profiler, so a prepare that
  fails to bind, or a prepared EXPLAIN ANALYZE that is never executed,
  leaves the flag behind.

The next query on the connection then runs as an EXPLAIN ANALYZE:
IsEnabled() is true whatever enable_profiling says, GetSaveLocation()
is empty, and EndQuery() emits nothing, so with enable_profiling and
profiling_output set, that query's profile is never written. It clears
the flag in turn, so one query is affected per EXPLAIN ANALYZE that did
not complete.

StartQuery() now clears the flag when the profiler is idle, before
setting it from the statement it starts, so a query is an EXPLAIN
ANALYZE only if its own statement is one. A running profiler keeps the
flag, so the StartQuery() calls made while planning or rebinding a
query that has already started are unchanged, and executing a prepared
EXPLAIN ANALYZE sets the flag again when it starts.

duckdb-r reaches this on every query: DBI's dbGetQuery() prepares and
then executes, and the glue runs all but the last statement of a
multi-statement query through Connection::Query()
(tests/testthat/test-explain.R).

v2.0-cyanoptera and duckdb/duckdb main keep the Reset() in Rollback()
and the early return in EndQuery(), and a duckdb-r build of
v2.0-cyanoptera leaks after the failures that happen once a query has
started. The same change fixes them there, but this hunk does not apply
as is, because StartQuery() resets the byte counters first, so a series
vendored from either branch rebases this entry rather than carrying it.
duckdb-r's v2.0 glue marks the profiler with StartExplainAnalyze()
before it executes a prepared EXPLAIN ANALYZE, and that mark still
reaches the engine with the change in place.

From duckdb/duckdb#21268 on, v2.0 starts the profiler when a statement
is prepared, and nothing ends it for a statement that is prepared and
never executed, so the profiler such a statement leaves is still
running, with its flag, when the next statement is prepared. On that
branch StartQuery() therefore clears the flag on every call before
setting it from the statement it starts: each call there either starts a
new query, and the parser marks an EXPLAIN ANALYZE once it has read one,
or names the statement it plans, so a call for a query that is already
running sets the flag that query has.

Proposed upstream in duckdb/duckdb#<PR> (placeholder: not opened yet)
against v1.5-variegata, and carried here until a release carries it.
---
diff --git a/src/duckdb/src/main/query_profiler.cpp b/src/duckdb/src/main/query_profiler.cpp
index 7d90107477..438fed5bfa 100644
--- a/src/duckdb/src/main/query_profiler.cpp
+++ b/src/duckdb/src/main/query_profiler.cpp
@@ -137,6 +137,12 @@ void QueryProfiler::Reset() {
 
 void QueryProfiler::StartQuery(const string &query, bool is_explain_analyze_p, bool start_at_optimizer) {
 	lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(lock);
+	// A query is an EXPLAIN ANALYZE only if the statement this call starts is one.
+	// EndQuery() clears the flag only for a running profiler, and a failed query's rollback resets it first,
+	// so a failed EXPLAIN ANALYZE, or one prepared and never run, would leave the flag set, and the next query
+	// would be profiled as an EXPLAIN ANALYZE, which writes no profiling output. Preparing starts the profiler,
+	// so the one left running by a statement prepared and never executed is still running here.
+	is_explain_analyze = false;
 	// Always reset byte counters at the start of each query so the progress bar shows per-query values
 	query_metrics.bytes_read = 0;
 	query_metrics.bytes_written = 0;
